7592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419578 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419578 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186419553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419578 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e00646f1-87a0-5a44-9d32-d7268a416e97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419579 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419579 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140056" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419579 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419579 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x+4y-2z \\\\ -3x-6y+3z \\\\ 4x+8y-4z\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419579 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "32b7b7cc-73cb-5678-9200-bd4242fb8265" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419579 #edb/ref 1074 "Finding the kernel of $T$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $T.$\n\nFror the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we find reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables, $y,$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nx=-2y+z.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,T$ is ${[\\begin{aligned}-2y+z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,y,z\\in R}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419580 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419580 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140051" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419580 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419580 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-x-2y-2z \\\\ -z \\\\ x+2y+3z\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ k \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419580 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "63f7892f-01e8-58a2-8307-26e6eb1dacb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419580 #edb/ref 1074 "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-(-6)-2\\cdot k-2\\cdot 0 \\\\ -0 \\\\ -6+2\\cdot k+3\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6-2k \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6+2k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6-2k=0 \\\\ -6+2k=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to these two equations is $k=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419581 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419581 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81393" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419581 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419581 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}9y-3x+6z \\\\ 3y-x+2z \\\\ 6x-12z-18y\\end{aligned}],$ find $\\text{nullity}(T).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419581 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aeb22a13-5f92-563c-bd00-38953af464b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419581 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -18 & -12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -18 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -18 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $2$ non-pivot columns (the $2$nd and $3$rd ones). Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419582 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419582 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80666" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419582 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419582 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-2x-14y \\\\ 3x+21y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,u\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419582 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f1fb1c9c-0f88-5161-9527-cc60a53751f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419582 #edb/ref 1074 "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2\\cdot 7-14\\cdot k \\\\ 3\\cdot 7+21\\cdot k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-14-14k \\\\ 21+21k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-14-14k=0 \\\\ 21+21k=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to both of these equations is $k=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419583 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419583 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38686" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419583 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419583 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a basis of $\\,\\text{Ker}(T)$ given that $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-y \\\\ 5x-3y \\\\ x+3y\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419583 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f93ed602-5802-58b3-b8ce-1b9e0f1d7153" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419583 #edb/ref 1074 "Finding the kernel of $\\,T\\,$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\,T.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & ↔{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -18 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{7}{18}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and $y$, and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we have $x=0.$ \n\nTherefore, if $x\\in \\text{Ker}(T),$ then \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]z,\\,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that $B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419584 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186419586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419584 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3d23af43-be13-585f-9055-f003ed10a6af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419584 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186419580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419585 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186419587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419585 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "acaaf24c-b820-5d27-9cad-c26f7f900e0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419585 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186419583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419586 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e5b6668f-23d9-5bd7-ab9f-9383a9c47d8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419586 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186419579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419587 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186419588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419587 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f4e671bf-aca9-5c31-b460-2f7da409a479" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419587 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186419582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419588 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186419584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419588 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fa869ace-3a7c-544c-91f1-9f8e4eedf28f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419588 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186419581 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
